From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 14:19:58 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: dt: leave out offline CPUs whose resources are not
 ready

The probe requests every present CPU's OPP resources first and defers
as a whole if any of them is not ready. A CPU that is offline may take
its supply or clock from a driver that brings it up later, or not at
all; the probe then defers forever and no CPU gets a policy.

On the MT6797 Gemini PDA the two Cortex-A72 cores come up from a module
that also provides their SRAM LDO, which is coupled with their supply.
The regulator core defers a consumer of a coupled regulator until all
its couples are registered, so without that module cpufreq-dt deferred
and the Cortex-A53 clusters went unmanaged.

Leave such a CPU out with a message. A CPU that is online, or that fails
for another reason, still fails the probe as before. A CPU left out has
no policy if it comes up later.
---
 drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c b/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c
index 1712b730a..6dc7b6349 100644
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c
@@ -312,6 +312,17 @@ static int dt_cpufreq_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	/* Request resources early so we can return in case of -EPROBE_DEFER */
 	for_each_present_cpu(cpu) {
 		ret = dt_cpufreq_early_init(&pdev->dev, cpu);
+		/*
+		 * An offline CPU's supply or clock may come from a driver that
+		 * brings the CPU up later, or never. Leave it out rather than
+		 * hold back the CPUs that are running.
+		 */
+		if (ret == -EPROBE_DEFER && !cpu_online(cpu)) {
+			dev_info(&pdev->dev,
+				 "cpu%d is offline and not ready, leaving it out\n",
+				 cpu);
+			continue;
+		}
 		if (ret)
 			goto err;
 	}
